# Recorded by hand from a reference runtime; see docs/reference-runtime.md.
# The model this refers to is NOT committed -- see docs/fixture-provenance.md
# for where to obtain it. No mandatory test reads this file.
#
# This one is here for the rule the 1B cannot exercise: a Gemma 3 of the
# 4B and up states a linear rotary stretch of eight, and that stretch is
# for the layers that see the whole context alone -- the windowed five in
# six turn as they were trained. Applied to every layer, the 4B answered
# a six-token prompt and came apart on a four-hundred-token one, a word
# repeated; the fixtures and the reference agreed with the engine because
# neither windowed and stretched at once. On the rendered prompt of the
# campaign's first task this engine now writes the same <tool_call>
# the reference runtime writes, byte for byte.
#
# Tokenization came from:
#   llama-tokenize -m gemma-3-4b-it-Q4_K_M.gguf
#     -p "The capital of France is"
#     -> [2, 818, 5279, 529, 7001, 563]
#   where 2 is the beginning marker this vocabulary asks for.
#
# Greedy continuation came from llama-completion with --temp 0
# --no-conversation, which printed " Paris." for the same prompt.
#
# The logits are the first three values of result_output from
# llama-eval-callback at the position after the prompt. The tolerance is
# 0.6, for the 1B's reason at thirty-four layers: this engine puts the
# second at -6.167 and a float32 pass over the exactly dequantized weights
# puts it at -6.167 too, the stretch on the global layers alone; the
# reference runtime sits 0.56 away, its activations rounded to eight bits
# before each product. The first agrees to 0.12, the third to 0.24.

runtime llama.cpp b10595-95b8e33e1
model gemma-3-4b-it-Q4_K_M.gguf
prompt The capital of France is
tokens 2 818 5279 529 7001 563
greedy_text \sParis.
logit 0 -10.5075
logit 1 -6.7284
logit 2 -0.0455
tolerance 0.6
